+# Contract: Evidence transcript (verification record)
+
+**Feature**: [../spec.md](../spec.md) | **Plan**: [../plan.md](../plan.md)
+
+Shape contract for the file the verification session produces:
+`docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-verification-<run-date>.md` (`<run-date>` = UTC date the session
+ends; house filename convention per
+[`install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md`](../../../docs/acceptance/install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md)).
+The field table in [data-model.md](../data-model.md) Entity 1 is the normative inventory; this
+contract fixes how those fields are *written*, so a reviewer can audit the record without the
+device.
+
+## Header (required, before any transcript line)
+
+1. **Title**: `# Windows 11 ARM64 verification — <run-date>` plus one sentence stating what was
+   verified (documented install journey + `graphhelm --version`) and against which
+   [`docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`](../../../docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md) revision.
+2. **Header table**, same shape as the existing acceptance records:
+
+   | Row | Content |
+   |---|---|
+   | Commit under test | 40-char SHA (B2), plus tag/PR if applicable |
+   | Host | OS name/version from B1 verbatim, `System type` line, CPU name, and the shell used (with a note that shell-reported architecture is context, not proof) |
+   | Started / ended | UTC timestamps taken at session start/end |
+   | Result | `pass` or `fail` (uppercase **pass** style per house convention) |
+   | Extra steps beyond the document | none, or each one named with its reason (V3) |
+   | Secrets | statement that none appear (journey is credential-free); if anything identifying was masked, the masking rule |
+3. **Provenance note**: physical device vs. ARM64-native virtualized instance (spec Assumptions:
+   virtualization qualifies only when ARM64 runs natively); no x86-64 emulation layer anywhere in
+   the observed chain.
+
+## Body (the transcript itself)
+
+- Every command as executed, `$`-prefixed, followed by its unedited output and `[exit N]`, exactly
+  the format the existing acceptance transcripts use. Order: Part B1 → Part A → Part B2–B5 as
+  executed (see [install-journey.md](install-journey.md)); the ordering followed must be
+  reproducible from the text alone.
+- The four anchor outputs are mandatory and non-elidable: the B1 identity lines, the B3
+  `rustc --version --verbose` block, the full Part A sequence, and B5's exact `graphhelm
+  --version` line with exit code.
+- Elision is allowed only for long toolchain download/install progress output and is marked
+  verbatim-style (`[... elided ...]`) — the same marker discipline the rehearsal record states up
+  front. No other edits. A transcript whose only elisions are unmarked has failed V5.
+- Tool failures inside the journey appear in place (command, error output, `[exit N]`) followed by
+  exactly the sanctioned remedy or a V3-flagged extra step; failures are never deleted from the
+  stream.
+
+## Footer (required, after the transcript)
+
+1. **Reading of the run**: which data-model validations (V1–V7) hold, each checked against the
+   transcript's own lines (cite the section, not memory).
+2. **Failure block** (only if `fail`): what failed, the cause as established by the outputs, the
+   environment state — this is the FR-007 blocker record, written so a later retry links to it
+   instead of replacing it.
+3. **Reference note**: the exact relative path GETTING_STARTED.md's platform statement will link to
+   (i.e., this file), and confirmation the claim text lands in the same change set (C3).
+
+## Style rules (governance)
+
+- English only (AGENTS.md documentation-language rule); prose minimal — the transcript is the
+  content, commentary stays in header/footer.
+- No secrets, tokens, keys, or API keys anywhere (S1); the journey needs none, so their appearance
+  is a defect in the record.
+- Paths in the record use the device's actual paths (they identify the environment); nothing here
+  must stay true on other machines, which is why no path is asserted as universal except the
+  document-relative ones the guide uses.
