+# Data Model: Verify GraphHelm CLI on Windows 11 ARM64
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](spec.md) | **Plan**: [plan.md](plan.md)
+
+The feature persists no application data — no tables, stores, or runtime state are created or
+changed (the spec's requirements checklist records the same: "no persistent user data"). What it
+*does* create and manage are two **repository records**, each with a fixed shape, validation rules,
+and a lifecycle. This file models them so the on-device session and the doc edit produce and check
+the same record twice (once written on the device, once read at review).
+
+## Entity 1: `VerificationTranscript` (evidence record)
+
+The durable acceptance record of one verification session. Created on disk as
+`docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-verification-<run-date>.md`; field shape defined in
+[evidence-transcript.md](contracts/evidence-transcript.md).
+
+### Fields
+
+| Field | Type / content | Source |
+|---|---|---|
+| `run_date` | UTC date, embedded in filename `<run-date>` | session end time |
+| `commit_under_test` | 40-char SHA (+ branch/PR if any) | `git rev-parse HEAD` on the device clone |
+| `os_identity.os_name_version` | e.g., `Microsoft Windows 11 ... ARM64` line | `systeminfo` |
+| `os_identity.system_type` | must contain `ARM-based PC` | `systeminfo` |
+| `os_identity.cpu_name` | processor name (expected to name a Snapdragon) | `Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor` |
+| `shell_note` | which shell ran the commands and its own reported architecture (context only, never the proof) | session |
+| `toolchain_identity.rustup_line` | rustup version output | `rustup --version` |
+| `toolchain_identity.rustc_host` | `rustc --version --verbose` output; `host:` line must be `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` | toolchain install step |
+| `pe_machine_check` | optional: PE machine field of the installed exe (AArch64) or a note "inspector not available" | R2 |
+| `commands[]` | complete ordered sequence exactly as executed, `$`-prefixed, each with `[exit N]` | session |
+| `outputs` | unedited output for every command, elisions only by explicit markers | session |
+| `version_output` | exact `graphhelm --version` stdout line + exit code | install step |
+| `result` | `pass` \| `fail` | session |
+| `failure_cause` | present iff `result = fail`: what failed and its cause | session |
+| `secrets_policy` | statement that no secrets appear (digests-only convention, no tokens/keys/API keys in scope) | session author |
+
+### Validation rules (a transcript is *qualifying* only if all hold)
+
+- `V1` (FR-003): `system_type` contains `ARM-based PC`, or `cpu_name` names a Snapdragon-class ARM
+  processor — OS-level identity, not a shell variable.
+- `V2` (FR-003): `toolchain_identity.rustc_host` shows `host: aarch64-pc-windows-msvc`.
+- `V3` (FR-001/SC-001): `commands[]` reproduces the documented prerequisites + section 1 steps
+  exactly; any extra command needed for success is explicitly flagged as a documentation defect
+  with the fix landed in GETTING_STARTED.md (spec edge case: toolchain absent).
+- `V4` (FR-002/SC-003): `version_output` equals the repository's expected version line for
+  `commit_under_test` (see research R6), and `graphhelm --version` exited 0 on first invocation
+  (SC-002).
+- `V5` (FR-006): all of `os_identity`, `commands[]`, and `outputs` are present and verbatim
+  (elisions marked), so a skeptic re-reads the journey without the device.
+- `V6` (S1/secrets): no secret values anywhere (journey is credential-free; rule stated, sweep
+  performed, mirroring the acceptance-record convention).
+- `V7` (FR-007): `result = fail` transcripts are recorded with `failure_cause` but make the entity
+  non-qualifying: they cannot be referenced by a verified-platform claim.
+
+### State transitions
+
+```text
+not_run ──(session executed, V1–V6 hold)──────────► qualifying_pass
+   │                                                
+   └──(session executed, some Vi fail)────────────► recorded_fail
+```
+
+`qualifying_pass` → referenced by `PlatformClaim` in the same change set. `recorded_fail` → claim
+stays withheld (or withdrawn); retry is a new dated transcript linked to the failed one, and a later
+green never erases the earlier red (AGENTS.md retry rule). There is no `qualifying_proxy` state:
+runs under x86-64 emulation or cross-built from another host do not enter this model at all
+(FR-003) — they are noted as context in the failing transcript, if anywhere.
+
+## Entity 2: `PlatformClaim` (documentation statement)
+
+The sentence(s) in [`docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`](../../docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md)
+section 1 asserting a platform is verified. One instance is added by this feature:
+
+### Fields
+
+| Field | Content for this feature |
+|---|---|
+| `platform` | Windows 11 on ARM64 |
+| `acquisition` | source build, using the section 1 commands shown directly beneath the statement |
+| `archive_statement` | untouched: "verified Windows and Ubuntu x86-64 archives" remains scoped to those archives (research R5) |
+| `evidence_ref` | relative link to the qualifying transcript (`../acceptance/windows-arm64-verification-<run-date>.md`) |
+| `edit_sites` | ≤ 2: section 1 statement; top-of-page citation paragraph only if the run qualifies |
+
+### Validation rules
+
+- `C1` (FR-004): statement names the platform, says how the CLI is obtained there, and keeps that
+  distinct from the archives statement.
+- `C2` (FR-005/SC-005): `evidence_ref` resolves within the repository to a `VerificationTranscript`
+  satisfying V1–V6. Every verified-platform statement in the file has such a reference.
+- `C3` (US2 scenario 2 / FR-007): the claim text and its reference land in the same change set as
+  the qualifying transcript; a review finds no claim without resolvable evidence.
+- `C4` (L1/L2): English-only prose.
+
+### State transitions
+
+```text
+UNVERIFIED ──(planning done, device observer absent)──────────► PENDING_DEVICE (OBSERVER_MISSING recorded; claim text absent)
+   │                                                        │
+   │                                                        ├─(qualifying_pass transcript)──► VERIFIED (claim + reference published together)
+   │                                                        └─(recorded_fail)────────────────► FAILED_HELD (claim stays absent; blocker named)
+   └──────────────────────────────(qualifying_pass, direct)──┴────────────────────────────────► VERIFIED
+```
+
+Current state after this plan: **PENDING_DEVICE** — `OBSERVER_MISSING: a native Windows 11 ARM64
+device on which the installed CLI can execute`. `FAILED_HELD` is reversible by a new passing run;
+nothing else writes to these states except a qualifying transcript landing.
+
+## Relationships
+
+- `PlatformClaim.evidence_ref` → `VerificationTranscript` (exactly one per claim; many-to-one is
+  legal — a future re-run may be cited by a later claim revision, but never while the old
+  transcript remains the cited one without both moving together).
+- `VerificationTranscript.commit_under_test` → repository commit (informational pointer; the
+  transcript itself is the evidence, the SHA anchors which version it proved).
+- No other entities exist for this feature; anything beyond these two records is out of scope
+  (research R7).
