+# Quickstart: Verification Scenarios — Windows 11 ARM64
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](spec.md) | **Plan**: [plan.md](plan.md)
+
+Runbook for proving the feature works end-to-end. The command sequences live in
+[contracts/install-journey.md](contracts/install-journey.md); the record shape in
+[contracts/evidence-transcript.md](contracts/evidence-transcript.md); entity lifecycles in
+[data-model.md](data-model.md). This file only says *which scenario proves what* and what counts.
+
+## Status of each scenario (kept honest per AGENTS.md JPD)
+
+| Scenario | Observable from this checkout? | State |
+|---|---|---|
+| A — on-device journey | **No.** This host is Windows 11 Pro x86-64 (AMD Ryzen 9 9950X; `systeminfo` reports `x64-based PC`). `OBSERVER_MISSING: a native Windows 11 ARM64 device on which the installed CLI can execute.` | Blocked on device — never reported as passed here |
+| B — documentation audit | Yes, once the change lands in the tree | Runnable by any reviewer |
+| C — blocked-path guard | Yes, right now on this host | Runnable immediately |
+
+A proxy (cross-compiling `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` here, running an x86-64 build under emulation)
+never satisfies Scenario A — the disqualifiers are listed in the install-journey contract.
+
+## Scenario A — Install and run on the device (FR-001…FR-003, FR-006; US1; SC-001…SC-003)
+
+**Prerequisites**: a physical Snapdragon-based Windows 11 laptop (or an ARM64-native virtualized
+Windows 11 instance), network access for the toolchain fetch, nothing pre-installed beyond what the
+document's Prerequisites table lists. One fresh PowerShell session.
+
+**Steps**: execute the fixed sequence from [contracts/install-journey.md](contracts/install-journey.md)
+(Part B1 first, then Part A, then Part B2–B5), writing each command and its unedited output into a
+transcript as you go, and finish the transcript per [evidence-transcript.md](contracts/evidence-transcript.md).
+Estimated time: one pinned-toolchain download plus a source build — comparison point: 186 s on a
+clean Ubuntu container; a laptop is similar.
+
+**Expected outcome**:
+- B1 shows `ARM-based PC` / Snapdragon CPU name (V1).
+- B3 shows `host: aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` (V2) — otherwise the fallback fires and the extra step is flagged.
+- Part A completes with zero undocumented steps (V3), and B5 prints exactly the document's expected
+  version line (`graphhelm 0.1.0` for workspace version `0.1.0`, at the tested commit) with
+  `[exit 0]` on first invocation (V4, SC-002).
+- The committed transcript passes V1–V7 → the data model's `qualifying_pass`, which unblocks
+  Scenario B's claim checks.
+
+**Count as failed** when any disqualifier in the install-journey contract hits; record with cause
+(FR-007) and keep the claim withheld.
+
+## Scenario B — Documentation audit (FR-004, FR-005; US2, US3; SC-004, SC-005)
+
+**Prerequisites**: a checkout containing the landed change (transcript + edited
+[`docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`](../../docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md)). Any platform.
+
+**Checks** (smallest that observe; run in PowerShell from the repository root):
+
+```powershell
+# B-check 1: the platform claim exists in section 1, names source build, and cites the transcript
+Select-String -Path docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md -Pattern 'ARM64' -Context 2,2
+# human review of the hit: claim present · acquisition = source build · archives sentence still
+# scoped to "verified Windows and Ubuntu x86-64 archives" (C1, US2 scenario 3)
+
+# B-check 2: the referenced evidence resolves in one hop (SC-004/SC-005)
+$ref = 'docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-verification-<run-date>.md'   # as linked in section 1
+Test-Path $ref   # expect: True
+
+# B-check 3: the cited transcript actually carries the proof (C2 → V1–V6)
+Select-String -Path $ref -Pattern 'ARM-based PC', 'aarch64-pc-windows-msvc', 'graphhelm 0\.1\.0'
+# expect all three anchors present, matching the contracts' required lines
+
+# B-check 4: no verified-platform statement lacks a resolvable reference, and prose is English-only (SC-005, L1)
+git diff --stat HEAD~1   # confirm the touched files are exactly: GETTING_STARTED.md + the transcript (+ specs/)
+```
+
+**Expected outcome**: all four checks hold → US2 independent test passes ("read the guide; follow
+the reference; it shows a successful install and passing `--version` on ARM64"). A claim found
+without a resolving reference fails SC-005 and the change must not merge (C3).
+
+## Scenario C — Blocked-path guard (FR-007; US2 scenario 2) — runnable now, on this host
+
+**Prerequisites**: nothing but this checkout.
+
+```powershell
+systeminfo | Select-String 'System type'        # expect: ... x64-based PC → not a qualifying device
+git grep -n "Windows 11 on ARM64" docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md
+# while no qualifying transcript exists in docs/acceptance/, expect: no match (claim not published)
+```
+
+**Expected outcome**: this host confirms the missing observer, and until Scenario A produces a
+`qualifying_pass` transcript, the guide does not state Windows 11 ARM64 as verified. This is the
+check that keeps a rushed doc edit from outliving its proof; run it again at any future point where
+the claim's status is in question.
+
+## Mapping back to the specification
+
+| Spec artifact | Observed by |
+|---|---|
+| US1 scenarios 1–3, FR-001…FR-003, SC-001…SC-003 | Scenario A (device) |
+| US2 scenarios 1–3, FR-004/FR-005, SC-004/SC-005 | Scenario B (after landing) |
+| US2 scenario 2 / FR-007 while the observer is absent | Scenario C (now) |
+| US3, FR-006 | Scenario A's transcript + Scenario B-check 3 (auditability after the device is gone) |
