+# Research: Verify GraphHelm CLI on Windows 11 ARM64
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](spec.md) | **Plan**: [plan.md](plan.md) | **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+Phase 0 output. Every entry resolves an unknown that the plan's Technical Context raised; none is
+left as `NEEDS CLARIFICATION`. Items that only a device can settle are decided here *with their
+device-time observation and fallback* so the on-device session executes a fixed plan rather than an
+open question.
+
+## R1. How the session proves the OS is Windows 11 ARM64 (FR-003, edge case: emulated PowerShell misreporting)
+
+- **Decision**: capture two system-level identities at the start of the session: (a)
+  `systeminfo` filtered to `OS Name` / `OS Version` / `System type`, where a native Windows 11 on
+  ARM reports `System type: ... ARM-based PC`; and (b)
+  `(Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor).Name`, expected to name a Snapdragon processor (the spec
+  allows either signal). Both lines go verbatim into the transcript.
+- **Rationale**: these values come from the OS's own identity services, not from the process's view
+  of itself. The spec names exactly this failure mode: a 64-bit x86-64 process (e.g., classic
+  PowerShell 5.1 running under emulation) can report AMD64 even on an ARM64 machine, so shell
+  variables are disqualified. `systeminfo` and WMI/CIM are present on every supported Windows 11
+  image, need no install, and work identically in PowerShell 5.1 and pwsh.
+- **Alternatives considered**: `$env:PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE` / `PROCESSOR_ARCHITEW6432` (rejected:
+  per-process values, the exact class of misreport the spec rules out); `Get-ComputerInfo`
+  (supersets R1 but takes ~10 s and dumps unrelated fields); registry `PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE` read
+  (same OS truth as systeminfo but less human-readable in a transcript); WMI via `wmic` (deprecated,
+  not guaranteed on newer images — CIM is its successor).
+
+## R2. How the session proves the executed binary was built for ARM64 (FR-003, edge case: wrong binary)
+
+- **Decision**: evidence by construction plus one optional direct check. During the documented
+  toolchain install the transcript captures `rustc --version --verbose`; the `host:` line must read
+  `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` — then `cargo +1.97.1 install --locked --path apps/cli` compiles for the
+  host target by default, so the installed binary is ARM64 and runs natively. As a secondary line,
+  when a PE inspector is available (`dumpbin /headers <exe>` showing `machine (AArch64)`, or the
+  2-byte PE machine field `AA64h` at offset `DWORD@0x3C + 4` of `graphhelm.exe`) the transcript
+  records it; absence of such a tool is noted, not fatal, because the host-triple line already
+  establishes the build.
+- **Rationale**: rustc's host triple names the compiler's own architecture; a native ARM64 compiler
+  targeting its own host produces a native ARM64 PE. An emulated x86-64 toolchain would print
+  `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` and is caught before any binary is produced. SC-003 (identical version
+  output across platforms) then holds by construction: clap prints `{bin} {CARGO_PKG_VERSION}` with
+  no platform term, and the workspace version is `0.1.0`
+  (root [Cargo.toml](../../Cargo.toml)), matching GETTING_STARTED §1's expected line
+  `graphhelm 0.1.0`.
+- **Alternatives considered**: file-size/hash comparison against an x86-64 build (weak: proves
+  difference, not architecture, and hashes differ between hosts anyway); `dumpbin` as primary
+  (not present on a clean Windows without Visual Studio tools — cannot be a prerequisite of the
+  proof); relying solely on "it ran" (unreliable: x86-64 binaries run fine under emulation and print
+  the same version — the spec's own disqualifier).
+
+## R3. Will the pinned Rust toolchain exist natively for ARM64 Windows, and does the documented winget path deliver it?
+
+- **Decision**: `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` is a first-class rustc target — Tier 2 since the beta
+  notes period and promoted to Tier 1 with Rust 1.91 — so the pinned `1.97.1` ships a native ARM64
+  toolchain, and rustup's distribution tree carries an `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` folder
+  (e.g., `static.rust-lang.org/rustup/dist/aarch64-pc-windows-msvc/rustup-init.exe`). The device
+  session therefore expects the documented winget command to yield a native toolchain — and it
+  verifies that expectation with R2's `rustc --version --verbose` immediately after
+  `rustup toolchain install 1.97.1 ...`.
+- **Fallback (fixed in advance)**: if the installed toolchain's host triple is
+  `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` — i.e., the winget package ran an emulated x86-64 rustup-init that made
+  an emulated default host — the session re-points with
+  `rustup set default-host aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` followed by reinstalling the pinned toolchain,
+  and records that extra step in the transcript. Per the spec's edge case "Toolchain absent on the
+  device", any extra step required makes the documentation wrong, and fixing
+  `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` (inside this feature's edit scope) becomes part of the delivery
+  rather than a blocker to it. The same rule applies to any missing MSVC linker the build demands.
+- **Rationale**: the research question was "can the documented journey succeed natively at all?" —
+  answered yes with a named escape hatch, so neither outcome opens a design change; both produce a
+  transcript plus, at most, an extra documented step.
+- **Alternatives considered**: cross-compiling `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` on this x86-64 host and
+  copying the exe to the laptop (rejected: violates FR-003's source-build journey and AGENTS.md's
+  proxy rule — it would also need the ARM64 linker installed here anyway); pinning a
+  Windows-GNU toolchain (different feature, changes the documented commands); assuming the winget
+  package is architecture-aware without checking (rejected precisely because R1/R2 exist to catch
+  the assumption on-device).
+
+## R4. Where and in what shape the evidence transcript is recorded (FR-005, FR-006, US3)
+
+- **Decision**: a new Markdown record at `docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-verification-<run-date>.md`
+  (date = UTC date the device run completes), following the house convention of
+  [install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md](../../docs/acceptance/install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md): a header
+  table (commit under test, host identity, start/end, result, secrets policy), then the transcript
+  verbatim as `$`-prefixed commands with `[exit N]` markers, unedited outputs, and elisions marked
+  explicitly. The exact required fields live in
+  [contracts/evidence-transcript.md](contracts/evidence-transcript.md).
+- **Rationale**: US3 says the transcript is kept "with the project's other acceptance records" — that
+  directory is `docs/acceptance/`; matching its filename pattern (`<slug>-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`) and its
+  header/verbatim style keeps the new record discoverable and auditable in one hop from
+  GETTING_STARTED.md (SC-004/SC-005) without inventing a new record format.
+- **Alternatives considered**: a JSON receipt (no precedent for journey transcripts in this repo;
+  the CLI's JSON envelopes serve the CLI, not journey records); a machine-neutral name without date
+  (breaks the existing convention and loses ordering against future re-runs after updates);
+  storing the transcript under `specs/` (wrong home: specs hold requirements/design, acceptance
+  holds executed evidence).
+
+## R5. What exactly changes in `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`, and why the archive statement survives untouched (FR-004, US2 scenario 3)
+
+- **Decision**: at most two edit sites. (1) Section 1: after the existing sentence stating that the
+  0.1.0 release provides verified Windows and Ubuntu x86-64 archives and directing other platforms
+  to a source build, add a sentence naming Windows 11 on ARM64 as a **verified platform**, stating
+  the CLI is obtained there by the source-build commands shown directly below, and linking to the
+  transcript (one relative hop). (2) Optionally, the top-of-page citation paragraph ("Every command
+  here was executed as written …") gains the new transcript link if and only if the run qualifies —
+  otherwise site (1) alone carries the reference and the page keeps exactly the references it
+  already had. The sentence about which *release archives* exist ("verified Windows and Ubuntu
+  x86-64 archives") is not rewritten, because publishing ARM64 archives is out of scope (spec
+  Assumptions).
+- **Rationale**: section 1 is where an ARM user lands and where the acquisition path
+  (source build vs. archive) is explained, so one-minute discovery (SC-004) and the required
+  distinction between "verified platform" and "existing archives" (US2 scenario 3) are satisfied at
+  the point of decision. Keeping the claim and the transcript reference in the same PR means no
+  revision can publish the claim ahead of its proof (US2 scenario 2, FR-007).
+- **Alternatives considered**: a new dedicated "Platforms" section or matrix table (cleaner at 10
+  platforms, over-structure at 3; larger diff, more prose to keep true later, and the spec bounds
+  the edit to this file's existing shape); editing INSTALL.md or the release notes too (explicitly
+  deferred by spec Assumptions to a later feature); wording ARM64 as an *archive* platform
+  (false: no archive exists yet — would invert US2 scenario 3's required distinction).
+
+## R6. Which version string the device run must match (FR-002, SC-003)
+
+- **Decision**: the run is tested against the repository state that GETTING_STARTED.md's
+  "Expected last line" block currently documents — `graphhelm 0.1.0` (workspace version `0.1.0`;
+  the CLI prints `{bin} {CARGO_PKG_VERSION}` via clap, no platform term). The transcript records the
+  commit under test and compares its `--version` line character-for-character against the doc's
+  expected block at review time. If the run happens after a workspace version bump, the comparison
+  baseline moves with it and the doc's expected block must show the same value — the check is
+  always "doc block ≡ transcript line at the tested commit", which is what FR-002/SC-003 require.
+- **Rationale**: pins SC-003 to an observable comparison instead of a memory of what the output
+  "should be". (The repository's top-level `VERSION` file reads `0.1.1-spec` — a release-cycle
+  marker not consumed by the CLI's version string, which derives from the workspace
+  [Cargo.toml](../../Cargo.toml); noted so the device session does not mistake it for the baseline.)
+- **Alternatives considered**: hard-coding `graphhelm 0.1.0` forever (stale after the next bump);
+  accepting "any semver-shaped line" (does not observe SC-003's character-for-character promise).
+
+## R7. What stays out of scope, per the spec, so the plan does not drift (Assumptions)
+
+- **Decision**: unchanged surfaces — all Rust code and tests, `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock`,
+  `INSTALL.md`, `docs/releases/0.1.0.md`, CI workflows, Studio. No ARM64 release archives are
+  published; no cross-compilation setup is added to CI; no `.github` workflow touches the run.
+  Extension hooks: no `.specify/extensions.yml` exists, so every hook (including `before_specify`
+  branch creation) is skipped and `BRANCH` stays `main`. Governance: no constitution file exists, so
+  `AGENTS.md` is the governance reference (recorded in plan.md Constitution Check).
+- **Rationale**: each item is a spec Assumption; restating them here keeps Phase 1/2 from quietly
+  widening the diff. Any device-run finding that forces a wider change (e.g., a prerequisite that
+  truly cannot be documented inside GETTING_STARTED.md) goes back through the spec, not around it.
+- **Alternatives considered**: pre-building CI cross-compilation to de-risk future archives (future
+  capability, different milestone); adding ARM64 to INSTALL.md in passing (scope creep by one file,
+  ruled out by Assumptions).
+
+## Consolidation
+
+No Technical Context field remains unresolved: language, dependencies, storage, testing, target
+platform, project type, performance goals, constraints, and scale were filled from measurements of
+this checkout (workspace `0.1.0`, x86-64 host, acceptance-record convention) and from R1–R7 above.
+The only fact still owned by the future is the device run itself, and it is scheduled with fixed
+commands ([contracts/install-journey.md](contracts/install-journey.md)), fixed expectations, fixed
+fallbacks, and a declared missing observer on this host.
