+# Feature Specification: Verify GraphHelm CLI on Windows 11 ARM64
+
+**Feature Branch**: `001-bench`
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-26
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "Confirm that the `graphhelm` CLI installs and runs `graphhelm --version` on Windows 11 on ARM64 (a Snapdragon laptop), and add Windows ARM64 to the verified platforms in `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Install and run the CLI on a Windows 11 ARM64 device (Priority: P1)
+
+A person with a Snapdragon-based Windows 11 laptop follows the documented installation path in
+`docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` (Prerequisites plus section 1) and ends up with a working
+`graphhelm` binary that runs natively on their machine. They confirm the installation by running
+`graphhelm --version`, which succeeds and prints the expected version identification.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the core promise of the feature — that Windows 11 on ARM64 is a
+working platform for the CLI, proven by observation rather than assumption. Nothing else in this
+feature (the documentation change, the evidence trail) has value unless this journey genuinely
+works end to end.
+
+**Independent Test**: On a real Windows 11 ARM64 device, run the documented commands exactly as
+written and observe (a) `graphhelm` resolves on the PATH after installation, and (b)
+`graphhelm --version` exits successfully with the expected version string. Delivers: a verified
+platform claim backed by reproducible steps.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a Windows 11 on ARM64 device that has only the prerequisites listed in
+   `GETTING_STARTED.md` Prerequisites, **When** the user executes the documented Windows install
+   commands (section 1) exactly as written, **Then** `graphhelm` is available on the PATH and the
+   install required no step beyond those documented.
+2. **Given** an installed `graphhelm` binary built for ARM64 running natively on the device,
+   **When** the user runs `graphhelm --version`, **Then** the command exits successfully and prints
+   the version identification for the version of the repository being tested, consistent with the
+   same version on the already-verified platforms.
+3. **Given** a candidate observation where either the operating system is not ARM64 or the running
+   binary is an x86-64 build executing under emulation, **When** the result is reviewed, **Then** it
+   does not count toward confirming Windows 11 on ARM64.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - The getting-started guide lists Windows ARM64 as a verified platform (Priority: P1)
+
+A developer deciding whether GraphHelm works on their Snapdragon laptop opens
+`docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` and sees Windows 11 on ARM64 named among the verified platforms,
+with a reference to the recorded evidence behind that claim. They trust the statement without
+having to test it themselves first, and any skeptic can follow the reference to the transcript.
+
+**Why this priority**: Documentation is the user-facing deliverable of this feature: the platform
+claim changes what new users believe they can do. It pairs with User Story 1 because the claim must
+carry its evidence.
+
+**Independent Test**: Read `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`; verify that Windows 11 on ARM64 is
+listed among the verified platforms and that the referenced evidence exists in the repository and
+shows a successful install and a passing `graphhelm --version` run on ARM64.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the recorded verification evidence from User Story 1, **When** a reader consults
+   `GETTING_STARTED.md`, **Then** Windows 11 on ARM64 is presented as a verified platform for
+   installing and running the CLI, with a direct reference to the evidence behind the claim.
+2. **Given** that the verification evidence has not yet been observed, **When** the documentation is
+   reviewed, **Then** it does not state Windows 11 on ARM64 as verified (a claim is never published
+   ahead of its proof).
+3. **Given** the released CLI archives described in section 1 cover specific architectures,
+   **When** the platform statement is written, **Then** it distinguishes how ARM64 users obtain the
+   CLI (e.g., source build vs. release archive) from the statement about which archives exist.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - The verification is recorded as durable, auditable evidence (Priority: P2)
+
+The exact commands executed on the device and their actual outputs are captured in a written
+transcript kept with the project's other acceptance records, including the device and system
+identities that prove the run was on Windows 11 on ARM64. The documentation references this
+transcript, so the claim remains auditable after the device is moved or the team member is gone.
+
+**Why this priority**: It protects Users Stories 1 and 2 against decay (the claim outliving its
+proof) but adds no capability of its own, so it is secondary.
+
+**Independent Test**: Open the recorded transcript in the repository and verify it contains the
+system identity proving ARM64, the complete command sequence, and the unedited `graphhelm
+--version` output; then verify the documentation's reference resolves to it.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a completed verification session, **When** the transcript is inspected, **Then** it
+   shows the device/system identity establishing Windows 11 on ARM64, every command as executed,
+   and the resulting output including the version line.
+2. **Given** the documentation names Windows ARM64 as verified, **When** its reference is followed,
+   **Then** it resolves to the transcript (or equivalent receipt) in the repository.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Emulated PowerShell misreporting the architecture**: on Windows 11 on ARM64, a 64-bit
+  x86-64 process (such as classic PowerShell 5.1) can report an AMD64 architecture even though the
+  hardware and OS are ARM64. The verification must establish ARM64 from the operating system's own
+  identity (for example `systeminfo` reporting an ARM-based PC, or the CPU identity naming a
+  Snapdragon processor), never from a shell variable alone.
+- **Wrong binary running**: an x86-64 build of the CLI runs correctly under Windows' x64 emulation
+  on an ARM64 device and would print the same version. Only a binary built for ARM64, running
+  natively, satisfies the feature.
+- **Toolchain absent on the device**: the pinned Rust toolchain for ARM64 Windows is not assumed to
+  exist. Installing it through the documented prerequisite steps is part of the journey, and any
+  extra step that had to be invented makes the test fail (the documentation is wrong, fix it).
+- **PATH not refreshed after install**: if the freshly installed binary is not found in the
+  current shell, the documented remedy applies exactly as on the verified platforms (new shell or
+  PATH refresh) and must not require anything more.
+- **Verification fails on the device**: the failure, its cause, and the environment are recorded;
+  the "verified platform" documentation change is withheld until a passing run exists. A failing or
+  unrun observation never becomes a verified-platform claim.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: The CLI MUST install on Windows 11 on ARM64 using only the steps documented in
+  `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` (Prerequisites plus the section 1 install path), with no
+  undocumented manual step required for success.
+- **FR-002**: After installation, `graphhelm --version` MUST run successfully on the ARM64 device
+  (clean exit) and print the version identification for the tested version, consistent with the
+  output produced by the same version on the already-verified platforms.
+- **FR-003**: The verification MUST establish that both the operating system is Windows 11 on ARM64
+  (native, evidenced by the system's own identity such as `systeminfo` "ARM-based PC" or a
+  Snapdragon CPU identity) and the executed binary was built for ARM64; x86-64 execution under
+  emulation does not satisfy this requirement.
+- **FR-004**: `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` MUST list Windows 11 on ARM64 among the verified
+  platforms, stating how the CLI is obtained there (source build) and keeping it distinct from any
+  statement about which release archives exist.
+- **FR-005**: The documentation statement MUST reference the recorded evidence (transcript or
+  receipt) behind the claim, in line with the repository's existing practice of linking
+  documentation claims to acceptance transcripts.
+- **FR-006**: The verification session MUST be recorded as a transcript capturing the device and
+  OS identity, the complete command sequence exactly as executed, and the actual output including
+  the `graphhelm --version` result.
+- **FR-007**: If the verification cannot be observed on a qualifying ARM64 device, the claim MUST
+  NOT appear in the documentation; the blocker is recorded with the missing observer named, and
+  the documentation change waits.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: 100% of the documented Windows install steps execute as written on the ARM64 device;
+  the number of undocumented extra steps required for a working install is 0.
+- **SC-002**: `graphhelm --version` succeeds on the first invocation after installation (no retry
+  needed) and prints the expected version identification.
+- **SC-003**: The `graphhelm --version` output on ARM64 is character-for-character identical to the
+  output for the same version on the already-verified platforms.
+- **SC-004**: A reader of `GETTING_STARTED.md` can identify Windows 11 on ARM64 as a verified
+  platform within one minute of reading, and find the referenced evidence with at most one
+  navigation hop.
+- **SC-005**: Zero verified-platform statements in the documentation lack a resolvable reference to
+  recorded evidence.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- "Windows 11 on ARM64 (a Snapdragon laptop)" means native Windows 11 on ARM64 hardware, physical
+  device preferred. A virtualized Windows 11 on ARM64 instance qualifies only if it runs ARM64
+  natively; any level of x86-64 emulation of the binary under test disqualifies the observation.
+- Scope is verification plus the documentation update plus the recorded evidence. Publishing
+  ARM64 release archives for the 0.1.0 CLI preview release is out of scope; the verified ARM64
+  path is the source build, and section 1's statement about which *release archives* exist is
+  left unchanged by this feature.
+- The documentation edit is limited to `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` (plus the new evidence
