+## Dependencies & Execution Order
+
+### Phase Dependencies
+
+- **Setup (Phase 1)**: No dependencies — can start immediately.
+- **Foundational (Phase 2)**: Depends on Setup completion — BLOCKS all user stories (both tasks fix inputs the device session consumes).
+- **User Story 1 (Phase 3)**: Depends on Foundational. No dependencies on other stories.
+- **User Story 3 (Phase 4)**: Depends on User Story 1 (its transcript body is written during Phase 3).
+- **User Story 2 (Phase 5)**: Depends on Users Stories 1 AND 3 — the entry condition is a `qualifying_pass` transcript (FR-005/FR-007).
+- **Polish (Phase 6)**: Depends on the desired user stories being complete (at minimum 1+3; 5 if the claim was published).
+
+### User Story Dependencies (completion order)
+
+```text
+Foundational ──► US1 (P1, device journey) ──► US3 (P2, transcript finalized) ──► US2 (P1, doc claim)
+                                    └──(recorded_fail)──► STOP: US2 withheld, blocker named (FR-007)
+```
+
+- **US1 → US3 → US2, not P1/P1/P2 order, is deliberate and spec-mandated**: US2 is P1 but its own acceptance scenario 2 and FR-007 forbid the claim landing before a qualifying transcript exists, and FR-005 makes the reference load-bearing. Sequencing the P2 transcript phase between the two P1 phases is the dependency the spec itself draws; nothing here re-prioritizes the stories, it orders them so the invariant holds.
+- **Within the chain**: tasks are strictly sequential inside each phase (one session, one transcript, one file at a time); only cross-phase-independent tasks carry `[P]`.
+
+### Within Each User Story
+
+- Identity capture (B1) before the journey (Part A) before build-result trust (B2–B3) before binary/version proof (B4–B5) — the contract's fixed order.
+- Failures are recorded in place, never deleted; a fail halts the chain at Phase 3's checkpoint.
+- Core observation before auditing; auditing before publication.
+
+### Parallel Opportunities
+
+- **Phase 1**: T001 and T002 are independent (T002 reads repo state, T001 reads planning artifacts).
+- **Phase 2**: T004 (baseline fixing from this checkout) is independent of T003 (device provisioning).
+- **Between machines**: while T003 runs on the device, T004 can be done from any checkout; T002 re-runs cheaply at review time.
+- **Phase 6**: T018 and T019 touch different files (specs/001-bench/quickstart.md vs. repo-wide state) and can run in parallel.
+- There are no parallel opportunities inside a single device session — it is one ordered PowerShell stream by design (contracts/install-journey.md).
